{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgsb2tgYfyWLm8mdtm7Kh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BSe6pYw2vmc5jUQZYfrBuf","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-1739780139/worktree/.pose/specs/2026-11-09-pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":342,"cache_read_input_tokens":17671,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":342},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"00a24e66-8ed0-413c-ad2a-7361ac819114","timestamp":"2026-11-09T15:25:54.462Z","request_id":"req_011Cgsb2tTQZbEcLUVwHzSxa","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01BSe6pYw2vmc5jUQZYfrBuf":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-1739780139/worktree/.pose/specs/2026-11-09-pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BSe6pYw2vmc5jUQZYfrBuf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tslug: pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules\n3\tstatus: in-progress\n4\tcreated_at: 2026-11-09\n5\tcompleted_at:\n6\tsupersedes:\n7\tdepends_on: pose-delegated-review-conclusions\n8\tremediates:\n9\tpriority: 1\n10\tcomponents: pose-mcp\n11\ttask_type: bugfix\n12\tdelivers: capability:review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t# Spec: The review brief states the rules the conclusion is checked against\n16\t\n17\t## 1. Intent\n18\t\n19\t### Goal\n20\t\n21\tA delegated reviewer that follows the brief writes a conclusion the attestation preflight accepts, whichever agent runs it.\n22\t\n23\t### Business value\n24\t\n25\tReviews by Claude Opus 5.5 were refused four times by the preflight although the review itself was sound: judgment criteria passed without a conclusion, evidence given as file and line, a criterion disposed as a finding without the id of a filed finding, a structural fact answered nowhere, tools answered `not-run`, a disposition that does not exist, with the required `artifact-check` and `surface-check` never run because the profile allowed no shell, and, once it could run them, tool evidence given as a bare command line where the engine wants `<class>:<ref>` and a sealed ref of the classes `surface-check` lists. The brief of `review-brief/3` named only `rationale` for not-applicable, `<class>:<ref>` for evidence and no `mappings` at all. Codex had stumbled on the same rules earlier in this repository and learned them from notes the implementer carried between rounds, and it reads `AGENTS.md` and the skills of the checkout, which `claude --safe-mode` does not. The gap is the brief, not the agent.\n26\t\n27\t### Constraints\n28\t\n29\t- The mechanisms are the ones POSE has: the brief template and its versions, and the delegation policy of the agent. No new mechanism, no new file.\n30\t- A run briefed with an earlier template keeps verifying and concluding: the brief a run was sent never changes under it.\n31\t- The reviewer's access does not widen beyond read-only commands.\n32\t\n33\t### Non-goals\n34\t\n35\t- Changing a rule of the preflight.\n36\t- Giving the reviewer a way to write, assess or validate.\n37\t\n38\t## 2. Requirements\n39\t\n40\t### Functional\n41\t\n42\t- R1: The brief of the current template shall state, for a run asked to conclude, each rule the four functions of the attestation preflight that judge a draft's criteria, tools, findings and evidence check before it records the conclusion (`validateBundleAttestationWith`, `reviewCriterionEvidenceBlockers`, `evaluateReviewToolCoverageIn`, `reviewToolEvidenceBlocker`): a passed judgment criterion carries a rationale; the evidence of a passed criterion is one of the refs listed under Sealed evidence; a criterion disposed as a finding carries the id of a finding of the draft; structural facts are answered as mappings by the criterion that asks for them; a not-applicable carries a rationale and is refused where the bundle seals evidence of the class the criterion asks for; every required criterion and tool is answered; a tool disposition is passed, failed, not-used or deferred, a required tool is run and passed, a recommended tool not run is not-used with a rationale, the evidence of a tool is a class:ref (a sealed ref of a class the tool lists, otherwise command: and the command run), and an approving draft leaves no finding open (each is a follow-up with action, owner and review_by, or the draft requests changes); and a draft that breaks one is refused whole.\n43\t- R2: A test shall tie each message those four functions can refuse a conclusion with to the words of the brief that state its rule, or exempt it with the reason a reviewer cannot break it, so that a rule added to the preflight without a sentence in the brief fails the build.\n44\t- R3: The template shall be versioned: `review-brief/7` is current (it adds the refusal of a draft that dispenses with every required criterion, and `/6` added the severities a follow-up can be accepted at, the deferred answer of a tool that needs the review complete, the component scoping of evidence, what a mapping's basis is and the one line outside the block that is read), `review-brief/5` (the identifiers of the findings a later round accounts for), `/4` and `/3` still render exactly as before, so a run briefed with it still verifies, resumes and concludes.\n45\t- R4: The delegation profile of the Claude reviewer shall allow, in its fresh, resumed and resumed-read commands, the required tools of a review plan (`pose artifact-check`, `surface-check`, `review-check`, `closeout-check`) and `git status` and `git ls-files`, and no verb that writes or runs a program: `pose assess`, `pose validate`, any `pose` verb that records, `git commit|push|add|checkout|reset`, `go build|install`, and the verbs whose flags write a file or run one (`git log|diff|show --output`, `go test -exec -c -o`, `go vet -vettool`, `pose lint-spec --json-out`), which a prefix allow-list cannot exclude by flag.\n46\t- R5: The manual shall say which template asks for the conclusion and that the brief states the rules.\n47\t\n48\t### Non-functional\n49\t\n50\t- The brief grows by one numbered item; the sealed bundle is unchanged.\n51\t\n52\t### Security\n53\t\n54\t- The allowed commands are the required tools of the review plan and two Git reads that take no flag that writes. The profile pre-approves those commands; Claude Code also runs, without asking, the commands it classifies as read-only itself (`cat`, `ls`, `find`, `git diff`, `git log`), and refuses a write (`touch` was refused in a probe). A prefix allow-list cannot exclude a flag, so a verb with a write or exec flag is not pre-approved; the reviewer reads the code with its read tools and relies on the sealed evidence for tests. The run safety record states that the worktree is not isolated from the filesystem, so no containment is claimed.\n55\t\n56\t### Compatibility\n57\t\n58\t- Runs briefed with `review-brief/3` conclude as before. New runs are briefed with `/4`.\n59\t\n60\t## 3. Technical Plan\n61\t\n62\t### Affected areas\n63\t\n64\t`internal/pose/review_brief.go`, `internal/pose/review_conclusions.go`, `.pose/policy/delegation.json`, the manual.\n65\t\n66\t### Artifacts\n67\t\n68\t- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-09-pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules.md\n69\t- created: .pose/starts/pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules.json\n70\t- created: .pose/changelogs/unreleased/pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules.md\n71\t- modified: .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n72\t- modified: .pose/policy/delegation.json\n73\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n74\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n75\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_test.go\n76\t- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\n77\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_versions_test.go\n78\t- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_preflight_tie_test.go\n79\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json\n80\t- modified: POSE.md\n81\t- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n82\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n83\t- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n84\t\n85\t### Delivery targets\n86\t\n87\t- capability:review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/cmd/pose/main.go\n88\t\n89\t### API/contract changes\n90\t\n91\tThe review brief template moves from `review-brief/3` to `review-brief/7` (`/4` stated the rules, `/5` the identifiers of prior findings, `/6` the accepted-risk severities and four more, `/7` that a draft cannot dispense with every required criterion); the reviewer profile `claude-opus-5-5` allows the required tools of a review plan and two Git reads.\n92\t\n93\t## 4. Tasks\n94\t\n95\t- [x] Find why the Claude reviewer's drafts were refused and the Codex reviewer's were not\n96\t- [x] State the rules in a new template version and keep the old one rendering\n97\t- [x] Tie the preflight blockers to the brief by a test\n98\t- [x] Allow the read-only commands of a review to the Claude profile\n99\t- [x] Update the manual\n100\t- [ ] Independent review and closeout\n101\t\n102\t## 5. Decisions\n103\t\n104\t### Decision D1\n105\t- Basis: R1, R3\n106\t- Minimal option: keep telling each reviewer in the note file\n107\t- Selected option: state the rules in the brief, as a new template version\n108\t- Rationale: the note file reaches one run and is written by the implementer, the brief reaches every run and is generated by POSE from what the preflight checks; a template version is the mechanism that lets the text change without changing what a run was sent\n109\t- Consequences: new runs carry one more item; old runs verify with the template they were sent\n110\t- Falsifier: a draft that follows the brief and is refused by a rule the brief does not state\n111\t\n112\t### Decision D2\n113\t- Basis: R4\n114\t- Minimal option: leave the Claude reviewer without a shell\n115\t- Selected option: allow read-only commands by verb in its profile\n116\t- Rationale: a reviewer that cannot run anything cites file and line, which the preflight refuses, and cannot reproduce the tests it is asked to judge; Codex runs under a read-only sandbox and can\n117\t- Consequences: the profile lists verbs; `pose assess` and `pose validate` are absent because they write generated files\n118\t- Falsifier: a read-only verb in the list that writes to the repository\n119\t\n120\t## 6. Validation\n121\t\n122\t### Strategy\n123\t\n124\tThe brief is rendered from a real sealed bundle and read for each rule; the older template is rendered and compared; the profile is probed with the same flags it is launched with: an allowed command runs and a write is refused.\n125\t\n126\t### Deterministic checks\n127\t\n128\t`go test -race` of the tests named in the trace; the strict matrix in a clean clone.\n129\t\n130\t### Requirement trace\n131\t- R1 [satisfied] test:TestTheBriefStatesEveryRuleThePreflightEnforces check:review-brief-conclusion-rules-integration\n132\t- R2 [satisfied] test:TestEveryMessageThePreflightCanRefuseWithIsStatedByTheBriefOrExempt test:TestTheBriefStatesEveryRuleThePreflightEnforces\n133\t- R3 [satisfied] test:TestAnEarlierConclusionBriefStillRendersWithoutTheRules test:TestReviewBriefIsRenderedFromTheBundleDeterministically\n134\t- R4 [satisfied] test:TestTheClaudeReviewerProfileAllowsOnlyReadOnlyCommands\n135\t- R5 [satisfied] test:TestEveryMessageThePreflightCanRefuseWithIsStatedByTheBriefOrExempt test:TestTheBriefOfALaterRoundStatesTheIdentifiersOfPriorFindings test:TestTheBriefStatesEveryRuleThePreflightEnforces\n136\t\n137\t### Known gaps\n138\t- The brief states the rules in words; it does not validate a draft before the run ends. A reviewer that ignores it is still refused by the preflight, as before.\n139\t- `delegation.json` is the policy of each instance. This repository's copy is changed; the other instances adopt the profile with `pose update` or by editing theirs.\n140\t- The brief rules (R1) are tied to the preflight by the words of the blockers in a test; a change to the wording of a blocker needs the table of that test updated.\n141\t\n142\t### Isolation of the secondary agents, reviewed at the end of this spec\n143\t\n144\tThe two reviewer profiles do not give the same guarantee, and the difference is of kind, not only of degree:\n145\t\n146\t- Codex has two command profiles: `read` (`-s read-only`) and `sandboxed` (`-s workspace-write`). The restriction is the operating system's. A review accepts only `read` access, so the profile difference does not enter a review; it shows in `delegate task`, where only Codex can be given write access.\n147\t- Claude has one, `read`, restricted by the agent's own permission system: `--safe-mode --restricted`, `dontAsk` and an allow-list matched by prefix. It has no `sandboxed` profile, and a prefix list cannot exclude a flag that writes a file or runs a program.\n148\t- In practice Codex runs `go test` and the plan tools inside its sandbox and was hampered by the read-only build directory and by a full validation that exceeded the 600 s of its run; the Claude reviewer cannot rerun tests (it reads the code and relies on the sealed evidence) and runs the plan tools. Neither agent was measured for time or cost; the Claude rounds were no slower than Codex's and failed more on the shape of the draft than on speed, which this spec addresses.\n149\t- Measured at the end of this spec: Claude Code offers an operating-system sandbox of its own (`sandbox.enabled` in the settings, which a profile can pass with `--settings`), built on bubblewrap and socat. `bwrap` is installed on this machine and `socat` is not: with the sandbox enabled and `socat` missing, the CLI reports \"Sandbox disabled: dependencies are missing\" and runs the commands unsandboxed, so enabling it without the dependency would claim a guarantee that is not enforced. Equivalence with Codex therefore needs `socat` installed and a Claude profile that passes the sandbox settings, checked at launch.\n150\t\n151\t---\n152\t\n153\t## 7. Final Report\n154\t\n155\t### Delivered scope\n156\tThe brief of `review-brief/7` states twelve rules (the tenth, the identifiers of prior findings, in review-brief/5; the eleventh group, the accepted-risk severities, the deferred tool, the component scoping, the mapping basis and the decision line, in review-brief/6) the engine checks on a typed conclusion and that a draft breaking one is refused whole (R1); a test ties each preflight blocker to the sentence of the brief that states its rule (R2); `review-brief/3` still renders as before and runs briefed with it conclude (R3); the Claude reviewer profile allows read-only commands in all three of its commands (R4); the manual names the template (R5). Left out: validating a draft before the run ends, and a change to any rule of the preflight.\n157\t\n158\t### Files and modules changed\n159\t- pose-mcp/internal/pose (review_brief.go, review_conclusions.go and their tests)\n160\t- .pose/policy/delegation.json (the Claude reviewer profile)\n161\t- POSE.md and its pt-BR and scaffold copies; the validation matrix gains `review-brief-conclusion-rules-integration`\n162\t\n163\t### Validation executed\n164\t- Command: `pose validate --strict --jobs 4` on a clean clone of the reviewed head, and the suites of `internal/pose`, `internal/cli` and `internal/mcpserver`\n165\t- Result: recorded by the sealed validation evidence; independent delegated review by a different agent, concluded with `pose review conclude`.\n166\t\n167\t### Residual risks\n168\t- A reviewer that does not read the brief still writes a draft the preflight refuses; the refusal names each broken rule.\n169\t- The reviewer runs the required plan tools and reads the code; it does not rerun the tests, which it takes from the sealed evidence.\n170\t\n171\t### Follow-ups\n172\t\n173\t- [open] Give the Claude reviewer a `sandboxed`-equivalent profile with the operating-system sandbox of Claude Code (`--settings` with `sandbox.enabled`), once `socat` is installed on the machine that runs reviews, and have the profile refuse to launch when the sandbox reports its dependencies missing, so that `go test` can be allowed again. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:medium review:2027-01-08)\n174\t- [open] Extend the tie of the preflight messages to the rest of what refuses a conclusion (the structural-causality and causality-closeout mapping rules, the finding-decision rules, the reuse rules, the round protocol and `ParseReviewDraft`), and state in the brief the rules found there: three or more mappings on one basis with one pasted rationale, a basis that reaches no requirement, a draft whose outside `decision:` line disagrees with its block. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2027-01-08)\n175\t- [open] Let `pose review conclude --dry-run` print the preflight blockers of a run's draft before the attestation is attempted, so a reviewer round is not spent on the shape. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2027-01-08)\n176\t- [open] Offer `pose delegate review` the same read-only verbs for the Codex profile as an explicit list, so both profiles state what a reviewer may run. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2027-01-08)\n177\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"52df41ed-ff8f-49e8-a305-994d1f8ef75f","timestamp":"2026-11-09T15:25:54.472Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-1739780139/worktree/.pose/specs/2026-11-09-pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules.md","content":"---\nslug: pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-09\ncompleted_at:\nsupersedes:\ndepends_on: pose-delegated-review-conclusions\nremediates:\npriority: 1\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: bugfix\ndelivers: capability:review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules\n---\n\n# Spec: The review brief states the rules the conclusion is checked against\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\n\nA delegated reviewer that follows the brief writes a conclusion the attestation preflight accepts, whichever agent runs it.\n\n### Business value\n\nReviews by Claude Opus 5.5 were refused four times by the preflight although the review itself was sound: judgment criteria passed without a conclusion, evidence given as file and line, a criterion disposed as a finding without the id of a filed finding, a structural fact answered nowhere, tools answered `not-run`, a disposition that does not exist, with the required `artifact-check` and `surface-check` never run because the profile allowed no shell, and, once it could run them, tool evidence given as a bare command line where the engine wants `<class>:<ref>` and a sealed ref of the classes `surface-check` lists. The brief of `review-brief/3` named only `rationale` for not-applicable, `<class>:<ref>` for evidence and no `mappings` at all. Codex had stumbled on the same rules earlier in this repository and learned them from notes the implementer carried between rounds, and it reads `AGENTS.md` and the skills of the checkout, which `claude --safe-mode` does not. The gap is the brief, not the agent.\n\n### Constraints\n\n- The mechanisms are the ones POSE has: the brief template and its versions, and the delegation policy of the agent. No new mechanism, no new file.\n- A run briefed with an earlier template keeps verifying and concluding: the brief a run was sent never changes under it.\n- The reviewer's access does not widen beyond read-only commands.\n\n### Non-goals\n\n- Changing a rule of the preflight.\n- Giving the reviewer a way to write, assess or validate.\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n- R1: The brief of the current template shall state, for a run asked to conclude, each rule the four functions of the attestation preflight that judge a draft's criteria, tools, findings and evidence check before it records the conclusion (`validateBundleAttestationWith`, `reviewCriterionEvidenceBlockers`, `evaluateReviewToolCoverageIn`, `reviewToolEvidenceBlocker`): a passed judgment criterion carries a rationale; the evidence of a passed criterion is one of the refs listed under Sealed evidence; a criterion disposed as a finding carries the id of a finding of the draft; structural facts are answered as mappings by the criterion that asks for them; a not-applicable carries a rationale and is refused where the bundle seals evidence of the class the criterion asks for; every required criterion and tool is answered; a tool disposition is passed, failed, not-used or deferred, a required tool is run and passed, a recommended tool not run is not-used with a rationale, the evidence of a tool is a class:ref (a sealed ref of a class the tool lists, otherwise command: and the command run), and an approving draft leaves no finding open (each is a follow-up with action, owner and review_by, or the draft requests changes); and a draft that breaks one is refused whole.\n- R2: A test shall tie each message those four functions can refuse a conclusion with to the words of the brief that state its rule, or exempt it with the reason a reviewer cannot break it, so that a rule added to the preflight without a sentence in the brief fails the build.\n- R3: The template shall be versioned: `review-brief/7` is current (it adds the refusal of a draft that dispenses with every required criterion, and `/6` added the severities a follow-up can be accepted at, the deferred answer of a tool that needs the review complete, the component scoping of evidence, what a mapping's basis is and the one line outside the block that is read), `review-brief/5` (the identifiers of the findings a later round accounts for), `/4` and `/3` still render exactly as before, so a run briefed with it still verifies, resumes and concludes.\n- R4: The delegation profile of the Claude reviewer shall allow, in its fresh, resumed and resumed-read commands, the required tools of a review plan (`pose artifact-check`, `surface-check`, `review-check`, `closeout-check`) and `git status` and `git ls-files`, and no verb that writes or runs a program: `pose assess`, `pose validate`, any `pose` verb that records, `git commit|push|add|checkout|reset`, `go build|install`, and the verbs whose flags write a file or run one (`git log|diff|show --output`, `go test -exec -c -o`, `go vet -vettool`, `pose lint-spec --json-out`), which a prefix allow-list cannot exclude by flag.\n- R5: The manual shall say which template asks for the conclusion and that the brief states the rules.\n\n### Non-functional\n\n- The brief grows by one numbered item; the sealed bundle is unchanged.\n\n### Security\n\n- The allowed commands are the required tools of the review plan and two Git reads that take no flag that writes. The profile pre-approves those commands; Claude Code also runs, without asking, the commands it classifies as read-only itself (`cat`, `ls`, `find`, `git diff`, `git log`), and refuses a write (`touch` was refused in a probe). A prefix allow-list cannot exclude a flag, so a verb with a write or exec flag is not pre-approved; the reviewer reads the code with its read tools and relies on the sealed evidence for tests. The run safety record states that the worktree is not isolated from the filesystem, so no containment is claimed.\n\n### Compatibility\n\n- Runs briefed with `review-brief/3` conclude as before. New runs are briefed with `/4`.\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n\n`internal/pose/review_brief.go`, `internal/pose/review_conclusions.go`, `.pose/policy/delegation.json`, the manual.\n\n### Artifacts\n\n- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-09-pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules.md\n- created: .pose/starts/pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules.json\n- created: .pose/changelogs/unreleased/pose-review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules.md\n- modified: .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n- modified: .pose/policy/delegation.json\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_versions_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_preflight_tie_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json\n- modified: POSE.md\n- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- capability:review-brief-states-the-conclusion-rules module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/cmd/pose/main.go\n\n### API/contract changes\n\nThe review brief template moves from `review-brief/3` to `review-brief/7` (`/4` stated the rules, `/5` the identifiers of prior findings, `/6` the accepted-risk severities and four more, `/7` that a draft cannot dispense with every required criterion); the reviewer profile `claude-opus-5-5` allows the required tools of a review plan and two Git reads.\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n- [x] Find why the Claude reviewer's drafts were refused and the Codex reviewer's were not\n- [x] State the rules in a new template version and keep the old one rendering\n- [x] Tie the preflight blockers to the brief by a test\n- [x] Allow the read-only commands of a review to the Claude profile\n- [x] Update the manual\n- [ ] Independent review and closeout\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Basis: R1, R3\n- Minimal option: keep telling each reviewer in the note file\n- Selected option: state the rules in the brief, as a new template version\n- Rationale: the note file reaches one run and is written by the implementer, the brief reaches every run and is generated by POSE from what the preflight checks; a template version is the mechanism that lets the text change without changing what a run was sent\n- Consequences: new runs carry one more item; old runs verify with the template they were sent\n- Falsifier: a draft that follows the brief and is refused by a rule the brief does not state\n\n### Decision D2\n- Basis: R4\n- Minimal option: leave the Claude reviewer without a shell\n- Selected option: allow read-only commands by verb in its profile\n- Rationale: a reviewer that cannot run anything cites file and line, which the preflight refuses, and cannot reproduce the tests it is asked to judge; Codex runs under a read-only sandbox and can\n- Consequences: the profile lists verbs; `pose assess` and `pose validate` are absent because they write generated files\n- Falsifier: a read-only verb in the list that writes to the repository\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\n\nThe brief is rendered from a real sealed bundle and read for each rule; the older template is rendered and compared; the profile is probed with the same flags it is launched with: an allowed command runs and a write is refused.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n`go test -race` of the tests named in the trace; the strict matrix in a clean clone.\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestTheBriefStatesEveryRuleThePreflightEnforces check:review-brief-conclusion-rules-integration\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestEveryMessageThePreflightCanRefuseWithIsStatedByTheBriefOrExempt test:TestTheBriefStatesEveryRuleThePreflightEnforces\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestAnEarlierConclusionBriefStillRendersWithoutTheRules test:TestReviewBriefIsRenderedFromTheBundleDeterministically\n- R4 [satisfied] test:TestTheClaudeReviewerProfileAllowsOnlyReadOnlyCommands\n- R5 [satisfied] test:TestEveryMessageThePreflightCanRefuseWithIsStatedByTheBriefOrExempt test:TestTheBriefOfALaterRoundStatesTheIdentifiersOfPriorFindings test:TestTheBriefStatesEveryRuleThePreflightEnforces\n\n### Known gaps\n- The brief states the rules in words; it does not validate a draft before the run ends. A reviewer that ignores it is still refused by the preflight, as before.\n- `delegation.json` is the policy of each instance. This repository's copy is changed; the other instances adopt the profile with `pose update` or by editing theirs.\n- The brief rules (R1) are tied to the preflight by the words of the blockers in a test; a change to the wording of a blocker needs the table of that test updated.\n\n### Isolation of the secondary agents, reviewed at the end of this spec\n\nThe two reviewer profiles do not give the same guarantee, and the difference is of kind, not only of degree:\n\n- Codex has two command profiles: `read` (`-s read-only`) and `sandboxed` (`-s workspace-write`). The restriction is the operating system's. A review accepts only `read` access, so the profile difference does not enter a review; it shows in `delegate task`, where only Codex can be given write access.\n- Claude has one, `read`, restricted by the agent's own permission system: `--safe-mode --restricted`, `dontAsk` and an allow-list matched by prefix. It has no `sandboxed` profile, and a prefix list cannot exclude a flag that writes a file or runs a program.\n- In practice Codex runs `go test` and the plan tools inside its sandbox and was hampered by the read-only build directory and by a full validation that exceeded the 600 s of its run; the Claude reviewer cannot rerun tests (it reads the code and relies on the sealed evidence) and runs the plan tools. Neither agent was measured for time or cost; the Claude rounds were no slower than Codex's and failed more on the shape of the draft than on speed, which this spec addresses.\n- Measured at the end of this spec: Claude Code offers an operating-system sandbox of its own (`sandbox.enabled` in the settings, which a profile can pass with `--settings`), built on bubblewrap and socat. `bwrap` is installed on this machine and `socat` is not: with the sandbox enabled and `socat` missing, the CLI reports \"Sandbox disabled: dependencies are missing\" and runs the commands unsandboxed, so enabling it without the dependency would claim a guarantee that is not enforced. Equivalence with Codex therefore needs `socat` installed and a Claude profile that passes the sandbox settings, checked at launch.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\nThe brief of `review-brief/7` states twelve rules (the tenth, the identifiers of prior findings, in review-brief/5; the eleventh group, the accepted-risk severities, the deferred tool, the component scoping, the mapping basis and the decision line, in review-brief/6) the engine checks on a typed conclusion and that a draft breaking one is refused whole (R1); a test ties each preflight blocker to the sentence of the brief that states its rule (R2); `review-brief/3` still renders as before and runs briefed with it conclude (R3); the Claude reviewer profile allows read-only commands in all three of its commands (R4); the manual names the template (R5). Left out: validating a draft before the run ends, and a change to any rule of the preflight.\n\n### Files and modules changed\n- pose-mcp/internal/pose (review_brief.go, review_conclusions.go and their tests)\n- .pose/policy/delegation.json (the Claude reviewer profile)\n- POSE.md and its pt-BR and scaffold copies; the validation matrix gains `review-brief-conclusion-rules-integration`\n\n### Validation executed\n- Command: `pose validate --strict --jobs 4` on a clean clone of the reviewed head, and the suites of `internal/pose`, `internal/cli` and `internal/mcpserver`\n- Result: recorded by the sealed validation evidence; independent delegated review by a different agent, concluded with `pose review conclude`.\n\n### Residual risks\n- A reviewer that does not read the brief still writes a draft the preflight refuses; the refusal names each broken rule.\n- The reviewer runs the required plan tools and reads the code; it does not rerun the tests, which it takes from the sealed evidence.\n\n### Follow-ups\n\n- [open] Give the Claude reviewer a `sandboxed`-equivalent profile with the operating-system sandbox of Claude Code (`--settings` with `sandbox.enabled`), once `socat` is installed on the machine that runs reviews, and have the profile refuse to launch when the sandbox reports its dependencies missing, so that `go test` can be allowed again. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:medium review:2027-01-08)\n- [open] Extend the tie of the preflight messages to the rest of what refuses a conclusion (the structural-causality and causality-closeout mapping rules, the finding-decision rules, the reuse rules, the round protocol and `ParseReviewDraft`), and state in the brief the rules found there: three or more mappings on one basis with one pasted rationale, a basis that reaches no requirement, a draft whose outside `decision:` line disagrees with its block. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2027-01-08)\n- [open] Let `pose review conclude --dry-run` print the preflight blockers of a run's draft before the attestation is attempted, so a reviewer round is not spent on the shape. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2027-01-08)\n- [open] Offer `pose delegate review` the same read-only verbs for the Codex profile as an explicit list, so both profiles state what a reviewer may run. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2027-01-08)\n","numLines":177,"startLine":1,"totalLines":177}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BSe6pYw2vmc5jUQZYfrBuf","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
